\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Flowchart of PACDIS. Left: the expensive-evaluation loop, with PAQC constructing the training groups and CDIS selecting the infill batch. Right: expansion of CDIS (dotted connector). A mode is drawn before the relation-guided search; $c$ counts the generated candidates and $g_{\max }$ bounds their accumulation. Indicator mode is drawn with probability $p_{\mathrm  {mix}}=0.5$ when its surrogate is available; otherwise the ambiguity-oriented mode is used. The selected mode determines the final filtering and selection criterion. The ambiguity-oriented mode uses a relation-quality screen followed by ambiguity-rewarded ranking; the indicator branch directly reranks its relation-screened subset. The batch is bounded by its cap and the remaining evaluation budget, without minimum-size completion. Detailed safeguards are given in Algorithms~\ref {alg:framework} and~\ref {alg:cdis}. }}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:framework}{{1}{4}{Flowchart of PACDIS. Left: the expensive-evaluation loop, with PAQC constructing the training groups and CDIS selecting the infill batch. Right: expansion of CDIS (dotted connector). A mode is drawn before the relation-guided search; $c$ counts the generated candidates and $g_{\max }$ bounds their accumulation. Indicator mode is drawn with probability $p_{\mathrm {mix}}=0.5$ when its surrogate is available; otherwise the ambiguity-oriented mode is used. The selected mode determines the final filtering and selection criterion. The ambiguity-oriented mode uses a relation-quality screen followed by ambiguity-rewarded ranking; the indicator branch directly reranks its relation-screened subset. The batch is bounded by its cap and the remaining evaluation budget, without minimum-size completion. Detailed safeguards are given in Algorithms~\ref {alg:framework} and~\ref {alg:cdis}}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{eq:label-bin}{{5}{4}{Representative-Based Grouping Signal}{equation.5}{}}
